Spring REST Getting Started

  This tutorial gets you from zero to a running REST endpoint.  Add one dependency to your
  <<<pom.xml>>>, write one YAML file, and your endpoint is live.  No controllers, no
  <<<@RequestMapping>>>, no Spring MVC configuration.

  {{{https://github.com/officefloor/OfficeFloor/tree/master/tutorials/springboot/SpringRestGettingStartedHttpServer}Tutorial Source}}



*Maven dependency

  The starter is published to Maven Central, so no additional repository configuration is needed.
  Adding a single dependency to <<<pom.xml>>> is all that is required:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/pom.xml|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*Application class

  The application entry point is a standard Spring Boot class with no OfficeFloor-specific code:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/SpringRestGettingStartedApplication.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*Your first endpoint

  An endpoint is a YAML file placed under <<<src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/>>>.  The file
  name encodes the HTTP method and the URL path:

+--
officefloor/rest/
└── greeting.GET.yml     →  GET  /greeting
+--

  The YAML file names the Java class that handles the request:

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/greeting.GET.yml|ignoreDownloadError=false}

  The handler is a plain Java class with no framework annotations on the class itself:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/GetGreetingLogic.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}

  OfficeFloor registers every Spring bean in the application context as a managed object.
  <<<GreetingService>>> is a plain Spring <<<@Service>>> injected automatically by type into any
  service method parameter whose type matches:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/GreetingService.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}

  {{{/apidocs/net/officefloor/web/ObjectResponse.html}ObjectResponse\<T\>}} serialises the object to JSON and writes it to the HTTP response.
  No <<<@ResponseBody>>> or <<<@RestController>>> is needed.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/GreetingResponse.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*Path parameter

  A path variable in the URL is expressed by a curly-brace file or directory name:

+--
officefloor/rest/
└── greeting/
    └── {name}.GET.yml   →  GET  /greeting/{name}
+--

%{snippet|file=${project.build.directory}/site-snippets/greeting/name.GET.yml|ignoreDownloadError=false}

  The handler receives the path variable as a <<<@PathVariable>>> parameter.  Always use the
  <<<name =>>> attribute form.  The shorthand <<<@PathVariable("name")>>> sets the <<<value>>>
  attribute; OfficeFloor resolves arguments from raw Java reflection where <<<@AliasFor>>>
  synthesis is not applied, so the shorthand silently produces an empty name and the binding fails:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/GetNamedGreetingLogic.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*Testing

  The application is a standard Spring Boot application, so tests use <<<MockMvc>>>:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/test/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/SpringRestGettingStartedTest.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}



*Running against a real server

  Because the application is standard Spring Boot, it can be run directly:

+--
mvn spring-boot:run
+--

  With the server running, the endpoints respond to plain HTTP:

+--
curl http://localhost:8080/greeting
{"message":"Hello, World!"}

curl http://localhost:8080/greeting/OfficeFloor
{"message":"Hello, OfficeFloor!"}
+--

  The integration test does the same thing programmatically.  <<<@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)>>>
  starts an embedded server on a random port and <<<TestRestTemplate>>> makes real HTTP calls:

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/test/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestgettingstarted/SpringRestGettingStartedRealServerTest.java|ignoreDownloadError=false}



